Subject: EVOLUTION/DIET: What proto-hominids ate 

Eric S. Raymond says:
> 
> Of course, before the 19th and 20th centuries, the average adult
> height was less than 5'7" and average lifespan less than 40.  The
> rise in meat consumption since the Industrial Revolution correlates
> well with the rise in height and lifespan.

Causes and correlations...  Being short is bad for your genes, if you're
male especially.  Tall people are respected and trusted more (look at
the average height of US presidents or UK PMs).  Or consider the phrase,
"look up to".  Salesmen tend to be tall.  Tall reporters get more
stories.  One of the stronger correlates of income is height (although
to further confuse things, intelligence weakly correlates with height
as well).  If you're a runt, well I'm afraid God doesn't like little
squirts and you tend to get shat on a lot.  Of course there are
disadvantages with height.  For one you require more energy, so you find
surviving famines a bit tough.  Having a high surface-area-to-volume
ratio (which tall people tend to have, in violation of the scaling laws)
makes winters a bit tough as well (maybe that's why Neanderthal was
short).  All-in-all civilisation is good news for tallies, bad news for
shorties.

I suspect that height is being sexually selected for, quite strongly,
now that we're civilised.  Height, like penis size (ever looked at Greek
frescoes, drawings.  Were those guys under-endowed, or what.....), is
running away with itself.  Just like jaw sizes are shrinking (albeit
slowly, as comparisons with Ancient Egyptians show) and give use
dentition problems.

That's not to say that diet is not having some effect as well, but we
should be cautious about drawing inferences solely from the environment
when genetic factors contribute as well.

